WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.545 --> 00:00:03.303
(light dramatic jingle)

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:05.643
- Hello, Believe Nation, I
started the Mentor Me Series

00:00:05.643 --> 00:00:07.901
with the goal to try
and hang around people

00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:09.838
who've had a lot more success than us

00:00:09.838 --> 00:00:12.488
and hopefully, by spending a
little more time with them,

00:00:12.488 --> 00:00:15.216
some of their mindset,
their belief systems,

00:00:15.216 --> 00:00:17.497
their way of thinking, seeps into us

00:00:17.497 --> 00:00:20.649
to help us become the
best version of ourselves.

00:00:20.649 --> 00:00:23.162
So today, we're going to learn
some of the very best advice

00:00:23.162 --> 00:00:25.687
from some of the Forbes billionaires.

00:00:25.687 --> 00:00:27.738
So every year, Forbes makes a list

00:00:27.738 --> 00:00:28.960
of the richest people in the world.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.387
Many of them are entrepreneurs

00:00:30.387 --> 00:00:32.095
and I thought it'd be a fun idea

00:00:32.095 --> 00:00:35.821
to take some of the very
best advice from the list

00:00:35.821 --> 00:00:39.301
of the wealthiest, most
successful Forbes billionaires

00:00:39.301 --> 00:00:41.409
to help us get stronger.

00:00:41.409 --> 00:00:43.077
And as always, as you're
watching the video,

00:00:43.077 --> 00:00:45.362
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:45.362 --> 00:00:46.945
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:46.945 --> 00:00:48.403
and put quotes around it as well

00:00:48.403 --> 00:00:50.803
so other people can be inspired, too.

00:00:50.803 --> 00:00:52.128
Enjoy.

00:00:52.128 --> 00:00:55.795
(light inspirational music)

00:01:04.447 --> 00:01:06.367
- When I started Microsoft,

00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:08.700
I didn't think of it as all that risky.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.960
I mean, I was so excited
about what we were doing.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:12.853
It's true, I could have gone bankrupt

00:01:12.853 --> 00:01:15.513
but, you know, I had a set of skills

00:01:15.513 --> 00:01:17.972
that were highly employable and, in fact,

00:01:17.972 --> 00:01:20.727
my parents were still willing
to let me go back to Harvard

00:01:20.727 --> 00:01:23.379
and let me finish my
education if I wanted to.

00:01:23.379 --> 00:01:24.759
- You've always got a job with me, Bill.

00:01:24.759 --> 00:01:27.253
(laughing)

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.914
- And that thing that was scary to me

00:01:29.914 --> 00:01:31.654
wasn't quitting and starting a company,

00:01:31.654 --> 00:01:34.136
it was when I started hiring my friends

00:01:34.136 --> 00:01:36.012
and they expected to be paid.

00:01:36.012 --> 00:01:38.262
(laughing)

00:01:39.245 --> 00:01:41.434
And then we had customers
who went bankrupt,

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:44.165
customers that I'd
counted on to come through

00:01:44.165 --> 00:01:47.138
and so, then I got this
incredibly conservative approach

00:01:47.138 --> 00:01:49.088
that I wanted to have
enough money in the bank

00:01:49.088 --> 00:01:52.000
to pay a year's worth of payroll,

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.541
even if we didn't get
any payments coming in.

00:01:55.541 --> 00:01:58.632
And I'm almost true to
that the whole time.

00:01:58.632 --> 00:02:00.883
We have about 10 billion now,
which is pretty much enough

00:02:00.883 --> 00:02:01.897
for the next year.

00:02:01.897 --> 00:02:04.717
(laughing)

00:02:04.717 --> 00:02:08.390
Anyway, if you're going
to start a company,

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:10.546
it takes so much energy

00:02:10.546 --> 00:02:14.379
that you better overcome
your feeling of risk.

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:18.899
I don't think that you, necessarily,

00:02:18.899 --> 00:02:19.951
if you're going to start a company,

00:02:19.951 --> 00:02:21.236
you should do it at the
start of your career.

00:02:21.236 --> 00:02:23.280
I think there's a lot to be
said of working for a company,

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.026
learning how they do things.

00:02:25.026 --> 00:02:28.345
If you're young, it's
hard to go least premises.

00:02:28.345 --> 00:02:31.658
They made that hard for
me, you couldn't rent a car

00:02:31.658 --> 00:02:33.957
when you were under 25 at the time,

00:02:33.957 --> 00:02:36.490
so I was always taking
taxis to go see customers.

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:38.662
(audience laughing)

00:02:38.662 --> 00:02:40.412
And people would say,

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.030
"We're going to have a
discussion at the bar."

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.274
Well, I couldn't go to the bar.

00:02:45.274 --> 00:02:48.274
(audience laughing)

00:02:52.458 --> 00:02:54.413
But you know, that's fun
because I'll tell you,

00:02:54.413 --> 00:02:57.135
when people are first
skeptical and they go,

00:02:57.135 --> 00:02:58.831
"This kid doesn't know anything."

00:02:58.831 --> 00:03:01.599
Then, when you show them you've
really got a good product

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:02.481
and you know something,

00:03:02.481 --> 00:03:04.268
they actually tend to go
overboard and they think,

00:03:04.268 --> 00:03:06.809
"Whoa, you know, they know a lot.

00:03:06.809 --> 00:03:09.573
"Let's really do an incredible
amount with these people."

00:03:09.573 --> 00:03:12.732
So our youth, at least in this country,

00:03:12.732 --> 00:03:16.736
was a huge asset for us once
we reached a certain threshold.

00:03:16.736 --> 00:03:20.236
It is hard, it's hard to hire older people

00:03:21.782 --> 00:03:23.399
because they'll be a
little bit conservative

00:03:23.399 --> 00:03:26.779
about whether they should
come and take the risk

00:03:26.779 --> 00:03:28.625
and it took three or four years

00:03:28.625 --> 00:03:32.194
before we could go out into
the normal employment pool.

00:03:32.194 --> 00:03:34.411
But those problems that
come with starting a firm,

00:03:34.411 --> 00:03:37.923
you better think of those
as part of the pleasure,

00:03:37.923 --> 00:03:42.316
part of the challenge, that
is part of the excitement.

00:03:42.316 --> 00:03:44.011
- [Woman] I was just wondering,
what would you consider

00:03:44.011 --> 00:03:46.391
to be the worst investment
that you've ever made?

00:03:46.391 --> 00:03:48.182
(audience laughing)

00:03:48.182 --> 00:03:49.396
- The worst investment I ever made?

00:03:49.396 --> 00:03:50.479
Hmm, how long do you have?

00:03:50.479 --> 00:03:53.378
(audience laughing)

00:03:53.378 --> 00:03:54.648
- I've made some very bad ones

00:03:54.648 --> 00:03:56.319
but that doesn't really bother me.

00:03:56.319 --> 00:03:58.555
You know, it'd bother the shareholders

00:03:58.555 --> 00:03:59.535
but that's another question.

00:03:59.535 --> 00:04:01.852
(audience laughing)

00:04:01.852 --> 00:04:03.989
You're going to make mistakes in life.

00:04:03.989 --> 00:04:05.548
I mean, there's no question about it.

00:04:05.548 --> 00:04:07.322
You don't want to make 'em on
the big decisions, you know,

00:04:07.322 --> 00:04:09.085
who you marry and some things like that.

00:04:09.085 --> 00:04:11.721
There's no way I'm going
to make a lot of business

00:04:11.721 --> 00:04:13.922
and investment decisions
without making some mistakes.

00:04:13.922 --> 00:04:15.600
I may try to minimize 'em.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.958
I don't dwell on 'em at all.

00:04:17.958 --> 00:04:19.073
I don't look back.

00:04:19.073 --> 00:04:20.740
The biggest mistakes

00:04:22.082 --> 00:04:24.402
are the ones that actually don't show up.

00:04:24.402 --> 00:04:26.728
They're the mistakes of
omission rather than commission.

00:04:26.728 --> 00:04:30.895
We never lost that much
money on any one investment.

00:04:31.911 --> 00:04:33.637
But it's the things
that I knew enough to do

00:04:33.637 --> 00:04:34.682
that I didn't do.

00:04:34.682 --> 00:04:38.849
We have missed profits of as
much as 10 billion dollars

00:04:40.543 --> 00:04:43.096
and things that I knew
enough to do and didn't do.

00:04:43.096 --> 00:04:46.484
Now, the fact I didn't
buy Microsoft way back

00:04:46.484 --> 00:04:48.370
is not a foregone opportunity

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:50.749
because I didn't know enough
to make that decision.

00:04:50.749 --> 00:04:53.492
But there have been other investments

00:04:53.492 --> 00:04:55.971
where I did know enough
to make the decision

00:04:55.971 --> 00:04:57.098
and for one reason or another,

00:04:57.098 --> 00:05:00.461
I either didn't do it at all
or I did it on a small scale.

00:05:00.461 --> 00:05:01.378
I was sucking my thumb

00:05:01.378 --> 00:05:03.358
when I should have been writing
checks, you know, basically.

00:05:03.358 --> 00:05:04.316
(audience laughing)

00:05:04.316 --> 00:05:06.633
And those don't show up.

00:05:06.633 --> 00:05:09.177
There's no place where it
shows missed opportunities

00:05:09.177 --> 00:05:10.802
but I've missed some big ones.

00:05:10.802 --> 00:05:14.866
The triumphs in life are partly triumphs

00:05:14.866 --> 00:05:17.124
because you know everything
isn't going to be a triumph

00:05:17.124 --> 00:05:21.581
and I would never get
too hung up on mistakes.

00:05:21.581 --> 00:05:23.146
I know a lot of people that
really agonize over them

00:05:23.146 --> 00:05:25.547
and it just isn't worth it.

00:05:25.547 --> 00:05:27.256
Tomorrow's another day
and you live it forward

00:05:27.256 --> 00:05:28.940
and just go on to the next thing.

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:31.451
- We know from our past experiences

00:05:31.451 --> 00:05:33.708
that big things start small.

00:05:33.708 --> 00:05:37.514
You know, the biggest
oak starts from an acorn

00:05:37.514 --> 00:05:40.264
and if you want to do anything new,

00:05:41.581 --> 00:05:43.905
you've got to be willing
to let that acorn grow

00:05:43.905 --> 00:05:46.460
into a little sapling and then,
finally, into a small tree

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:51.176
and maybe one day, it'll be
a big business on its own.

00:05:51.176 --> 00:05:52.844
- And in fact, that's one of the mottoes

00:05:52.844 --> 00:05:55.378
for one of your initiatives
and forgive my pronunciation

00:05:55.378 --> 00:05:58.327
of the latin, Gradatim ferociter,

00:05:58.327 --> 00:05:59.591
what does that mean to you?

00:05:59.591 --> 00:06:03.498
- Well it means step-by-step ferociously

00:06:03.498 --> 00:06:06.710
and it's the motto for Blue Origin.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:09.710
And basically, you can't skip steps.

00:06:11.569 --> 00:06:13.469
You have to put one foot
in front of the other.

00:06:13.469 --> 00:06:15.396
Things take time.

00:06:15.396 --> 00:06:17.313
There are no shortcuts.

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:21.351
But you want to do those steps with,

00:06:21.351 --> 00:06:24.658
you know, passion and ferocity.

00:06:24.658 --> 00:06:27.660
- The main thing is, when
you're starting something,

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:30.910
it's just kind of hard, you
need to be pretty headstrong

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:32.537
about it, where there are going
to be all these challenges

00:06:32.537 --> 00:06:34.957
that come up and I think the
main thing that you need to do

00:06:34.957 --> 00:06:36.335
is just not give up, right?

00:06:36.335 --> 00:06:38.147
And kind of know what you want to do

00:06:38.147 --> 00:06:41.474
and the best entrepreneurs who I've met

00:06:41.474 --> 00:06:43.955
don't really start
companies because their goal

00:06:43.955 --> 00:06:46.000
is to build a company, they do it

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:47.439
because they want to make
a change in the world

00:06:47.439 --> 00:06:50.808
and help people and I think, if
you kind of stay true to that,

00:06:50.808 --> 00:06:54.554
and if you just focus on powering through,

00:06:54.554 --> 00:06:56.136
no matter what the challenges are

00:06:56.136 --> 00:06:58.482
that will inevitably come up in your path,

00:06:58.482 --> 00:07:00.503
then you'll find that
there are lots of tools

00:07:00.503 --> 00:07:01.942
that are available and a lot of people

00:07:01.942 --> 00:07:03.714
who will help you build
what you're building.

00:07:03.714 --> 00:07:04.809
- Think things out for yourself.

00:07:04.809 --> 00:07:05.815
Come to your own judgements.

00:07:05.815 --> 00:07:09.982
Don't simply conform to
conventional ways of thinking,

00:07:11.653 --> 00:07:15.415
conventional ways of dressing,
conventional ways of acting.

00:07:15.415 --> 00:07:18.498
A lot of things are based on fashion.

00:07:19.919 --> 00:07:24.352
Even morality, at times,
is based on fashion.

00:07:24.352 --> 00:07:28.303
Slavery was once considered
not to be immoral.

00:07:28.303 --> 00:07:32.470
People are shocked that the
Ancient Greeks had slaves,

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:35.719
that we had slavery in
this country as recently

00:07:35.719 --> 00:07:38.373
as 130, 140 years ago.

00:07:38.373 --> 00:07:41.335
So, there are moral values,
you have to really go back

00:07:41.335 --> 00:07:44.083
to first principles and think
things out for yourself,

00:07:44.083 --> 00:07:46.716
whether they're scientific
principles or moral principles

00:07:46.716 --> 00:07:49.185
or business ideas or product ideas,

00:07:49.185 --> 00:07:51.187
you have to think things out for yourself.

00:07:51.187 --> 00:07:55.270
- I believe that the family
and the personal life

00:07:58.317 --> 00:08:00.984
in business or professional life

00:08:02.132 --> 00:08:05.253
is not totally compatible, it's necessary.

00:08:05.253 --> 00:08:08.713
You will have a better professional life

00:08:08.713 --> 00:08:12.130
with a good personal life and family life

00:08:13.569 --> 00:08:17.736
than if you don't have a good
personal life and family life.

00:08:19.828 --> 00:08:23.795
I don't understand the
contrary and it's very clear

00:08:23.795 --> 00:08:27.712
that the good personal
life makes you stronger,

00:08:33.348 --> 00:08:35.828
you are stronger for the challenges,

00:08:35.828 --> 00:08:39.995
you are working, I don't
believe in working the hours

00:08:42.918 --> 00:08:45.504
of 16 hours or 14 hours.

00:08:45.504 --> 00:08:48.674
When you work too much,
it's because your job

00:08:48.674 --> 00:08:49.841
is beyond you.

00:08:51.783 --> 00:08:56.767
You are not controlling, you
are not doing good, your job.

00:08:56.767 --> 00:08:58.851
If you need to be 15 hours, 16 hours,

00:08:58.851 --> 00:09:01.298
it's because you are not organized,

00:09:01.298 --> 00:09:04.909
that you don't have delegation
of your responsibilities

00:09:04.909 --> 00:09:09.076
and I don't believe in any
business, in any activity,

00:09:11.237 --> 00:09:13.238
you need to work 15 or 16 hours

00:09:13.238 --> 00:09:14.857
and you don't have time for yourself

00:09:14.857 --> 00:09:16.888
or for your family or for anyone.

00:09:16.888 --> 00:09:21.676
- When I graduated from
universities, and before I,

00:09:21.676 --> 00:09:24.701
you know, for three years I
tried for in the universities,

00:09:24.701 --> 00:09:28.618
so applied jobs for thirty
times, got rejected.

00:09:29.605 --> 00:09:32.382
I went for a place, they
said, "No, you're not good."

00:09:32.382 --> 00:09:36.459
I went to even the KFC,
when KFC came to China,

00:09:36.459 --> 00:09:38.173
came to our city,
(audience laughing)

00:09:38.173 --> 00:09:40.423
24 people went for the job.

00:09:42.905 --> 00:09:44.819
23 people were accepted,

00:09:44.819 --> 00:09:47.167
I was the only guy.
(audience laughing)

00:09:47.167 --> 00:09:49.449
And we went for police, five people,

00:09:49.449 --> 00:09:50.744
four of them accepted,

00:09:50.744 --> 00:09:53.154
I was the only guy that
did not receive it.

00:09:53.154 --> 00:09:56.374
So to me, being turned down, rejected,

00:09:56.374 --> 00:09:59.886
oh by the way, I told
you I applied for Harvard

00:09:59.886 --> 00:10:01.309
for 10 times, rejected.

00:10:01.309 --> 00:10:02.607
(audience laughing)

00:10:02.607 --> 00:10:06.720
I know I'll be rejected--
- They're sorry now.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:07.825
10 times, your wrote them

00:10:07.825 --> 00:10:10.458
and said "I'd like to come to Harvard."

00:10:10.458 --> 00:10:11.995
- Yeah, and then I told myself,

00:10:11.995 --> 00:10:13.759
"Someday, I should go teach there, buddy."

00:10:13.759 --> 00:10:15.624
(audience laughing)

00:10:15.624 --> 00:10:17.702
- Now, if you want to make a quick buck,

00:10:17.702 --> 00:10:20.674
you can do it a lot of different ways.

00:10:20.674 --> 00:10:24.841
You can cheat somebody, you
can misrepresent something,

00:10:27.486 --> 00:10:31.039
you can go manipulate the political system

00:10:31.039 --> 00:10:34.945
to get an advantage, but if
you want to be successful

00:10:34.945 --> 00:10:36.615
over a long period of time,

00:10:36.615 --> 00:10:40.421
I believe you need to focus first of all

00:10:40.421 --> 00:10:42.785
on creating value for others.

00:10:42.785 --> 00:10:46.952
And I've had people, "Oh,
that's naive, that's utopian."

00:10:49.197 --> 00:10:51.966
No, it isn't, because
why will the customers

00:10:51.966 --> 00:10:54.809
want to pay you anything
over a long period

00:10:54.809 --> 00:10:58.102
unless you're creating value for them?

00:10:58.102 --> 00:11:02.484
Why would your employees
want to work for you

00:11:02.484 --> 00:11:05.192
or if they did, they didn't
have any alternative,

00:11:05.192 --> 00:11:08.283
why do they want to
give their best efforts?

00:11:08.283 --> 00:11:09.866
Why do they want to get excited?

00:11:09.866 --> 00:11:13.474
Why would they wake up
at night with ideas?

00:11:13.474 --> 00:11:16.371
If you're in a community,
a plant in a community,

00:11:16.371 --> 00:11:19.029
unless you're creating
value for that community,

00:11:19.029 --> 00:11:21.293
why would the community want you there?

00:11:21.293 --> 00:11:23.692
If you're polluting and hurting people

00:11:23.692 --> 00:11:26.567
and not contributing
anything to the community,

00:11:26.567 --> 00:11:31.127
so long term success
starts with being dedicated

00:11:31.127 --> 00:11:33.636
to creating value for others.

00:11:33.636 --> 00:11:36.803
It's not altruism, my whole philosophy

00:11:39.184 --> 00:11:42.351
is to have a system of mutual benefit,

00:11:43.395 --> 00:11:47.562
where both parties gain, a
society based on win-win.

00:11:49.094 --> 00:11:52.177
- The only thing that you can control

00:11:53.827 --> 00:11:57.994
that influences success in
life is how hard you work,

00:11:59.201 --> 00:12:03.629
how honest you are and how
well you deal with others.

00:12:03.629 --> 00:12:06.652
You can control, those are
variables, those variables.

00:12:06.652 --> 00:12:08.778
You can't control how lucky you are,

00:12:08.778 --> 00:12:10.570
although, the more you
work, the luckier you get.

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:14.762
You have no influence on the
intellectual capabilities

00:12:14.762 --> 00:12:17.035
that God did or did not give you

00:12:17.035 --> 00:12:19.932
but you can work as hard as possible,

00:12:19.932 --> 00:12:23.355
you can be scrupulously
honest so people respect you

00:12:23.355 --> 00:12:25.254
and you can get along with others

00:12:25.254 --> 00:12:27.389
because nobody does
anything by themselves.

00:12:27.389 --> 00:12:29.605
- Did you have to pay a
price, though, you personally?

00:12:29.605 --> 00:12:32.325
- Well, I think that
everything is an either/or.

00:12:32.325 --> 00:12:34.031
There is a price in everything.

00:12:34.031 --> 00:12:37.451
You can study or you can go out and play.

00:12:37.451 --> 00:12:41.667
You can defer to others or
you can try to hog the credit.

00:12:41.667 --> 00:12:46.045
You can be honest and
suffer the consequences

00:12:46.045 --> 00:12:47.859
or you can lie, cheat, and steal

00:12:47.859 --> 00:12:51.510
and short-term, maybe even
advance but long-term,

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:53.415
you are better off if you work harder.

00:12:53.415 --> 00:12:54.843
You are better off if you're honest

00:12:54.843 --> 00:12:57.055
and you are better off
if you give others credit

00:12:57.055 --> 00:12:59.624
and get them to help you, by helping them.

00:12:59.624 --> 00:13:02.060
- What do you tell others who
try to strike that balance?

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:04.873
Between family and work
and volunteer efforts

00:13:04.873 --> 00:13:06.147
and everything else?
- Well, you have to answer

00:13:06.147 --> 00:13:08.586
and either/or is invariably yes,

00:13:08.586 --> 00:13:10.349
you can't go all in one direction.

00:13:10.349 --> 00:13:14.516
You cannot walk away from
your family for your career.

00:13:15.613 --> 00:13:17.700
On the other hand, if you
walk away from your career

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:20.352
for your family, you're
not going to have the money

00:13:20.352 --> 00:13:23.068
to support the family and
parents, all the time,

00:13:23.068 --> 00:13:25.979
are making those kinds of choices

00:13:25.979 --> 00:13:30.141
between career and family,
they do some of both

00:13:30.141 --> 00:13:32.178
and you try to blend the two

00:13:32.178 --> 00:13:34.008
and that may not be the perfect answer

00:13:34.008 --> 00:13:36.509
but that's a practical answer

00:13:36.509 --> 00:13:39.512
that I've always told young people

00:13:39.512 --> 00:13:41.936
when they go off to
college, you have to study

00:13:41.936 --> 00:13:44.738
and you have to get good grades

00:13:44.738 --> 00:13:47.681
but you certainly should
have a good time, as well.

00:13:47.681 --> 00:13:50.411
Because that's part of
your growing up experience

00:13:50.411 --> 00:13:54.963
and there's no point in leading
life to suffer all the time,

00:13:54.963 --> 00:13:58.360
you want to enjoy life as
well as the next person.

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:01.691
So, it's some balance,
some blend between the two.

00:14:01.691 --> 00:14:04.490
- I want to talk about
dreams for a second.

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:08.699
And in my case, we're
literally in a dream.

00:14:08.699 --> 00:14:12.955
When I was in college, I was
sure that I'd been admitted

00:14:12.955 --> 00:14:17.104
by a clerical error,
probably a computer error.

00:14:17.104 --> 00:14:18.918
(audience laughing)
And because of that,

00:14:18.918 --> 00:14:23.883
I had an irrational fear
I'd be sent home on the bus.

00:14:23.883 --> 00:14:27.415
The Sergei's here knows this is true.

00:14:27.415 --> 00:14:28.573
(audience laughing)

00:14:28.573 --> 00:14:31.023
But it turns out, because of that anxiety,

00:14:31.023 --> 00:14:33.690
I woke up literally with a dream

00:14:34.655 --> 00:14:36.749
and it was kind of a strange dream.

00:14:36.749 --> 00:14:41.485
It went like, I think I
can download the entire web

00:14:41.485 --> 00:14:43.777
onto some old computers
that were lying around.

00:14:43.777 --> 00:14:45.239
(audience laughing)

00:14:45.239 --> 00:14:49.604
And that would probably seem
pretty crazy to most people

00:14:49.604 --> 00:14:52.473
but I stayed up a couple hours
in the middle of the night

00:14:52.473 --> 00:14:57.427
doing some math and it seemed
actually pretty plausible.

00:14:57.427 --> 00:15:00.700
Well, assuming you actually
didn't keep any of the webpages,

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:02.700
you only kept the links.

00:15:03.579 --> 00:15:06.814
And then, started to figure
out, given all that data,

00:15:06.814 --> 00:15:09.626
I thought it would take a couple of weeks

00:15:09.626 --> 00:15:13.793
and I told my advisor that and
he just sort of laughed at me

00:15:14.957 --> 00:15:18.094
and it of course took a year or two.

00:15:18.094 --> 00:15:22.828
But at the end of that, we
had a way to rank webpages

00:15:22.828 --> 00:15:25.173
and no thoughts to search it all,

00:15:25.173 --> 00:15:28.292
but eventually, search entered the picture

00:15:28.292 --> 00:15:31.546
and you know the rest
and that became Google.

00:15:31.546 --> 00:15:33.545
(audience applauding)

00:15:33.545 --> 00:15:37.712
So I hope to encourage everyone
to follow their dreams.

00:15:39.801 --> 00:15:40.867
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:15:40.867 --> 00:15:42.723
I hope you enjoyed, I'd love to know,

00:15:42.723 --> 00:15:45.079
what did you think of this
video, did you enjoy it?

00:15:45.079 --> 00:15:45.964
Did you learn from it?

00:15:45.964 --> 00:15:48.674
What was your favorite clip and
what was the biggest message

00:15:48.674 --> 00:15:50.673
that you took from this video?

00:15:50.673 --> 00:15:52.884
What are you going to
immediately apply to your life

00:15:52.884 --> 00:15:54.004
or to your business?

00:15:54.004 --> 00:15:55.277
Leave it down in the comments below

00:15:55.277 --> 00:15:57.198
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:15:57.198 --> 00:15:58.675
Finally, I also want to
give a quick shout out

00:15:58.675 --> 00:16:01.085
to Christophe, thank you so
much for picking up a copy

00:16:01.085 --> 00:16:04.662
of my book, Your One Word,
and posting that fun picture

00:16:04.662 --> 00:16:08.024
on Instagram that included the
little tiny temo on the side,

00:16:08.024 --> 00:16:10.781
I really, really, really
appreciate your support.

00:16:10.781 --> 00:16:12.074
So, thank you guys again for watching.

00:16:12.074 --> 00:16:13.290
I believe in you.

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:15.368
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:16:15.368 --> 00:16:19.960
and whatever your one word is,
much love, I'll see you soon.

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:23.210
(light dramatic music)

00:16:25.859 --> 00:16:29.781
- I want us happy focused on
short-term money coming in

00:16:29.781 --> 00:16:31.288
when creating a company because otherwise,

00:16:31.288 --> 00:16:33.325
the company will die.

00:16:33.325 --> 00:16:37.886
So, I think that a lot of
times, people think like,

00:16:37.886 --> 00:16:41.120
creating a company is going to be fun.

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:42.925
I would say it's really not that fun.

00:16:42.925 --> 00:16:44.534
Well, there are periods of fun

00:16:44.534 --> 00:16:48.468
and there are periods of
where it is just awful.

00:16:48.468 --> 00:16:51.061
And particularly, if you
oversee over a company,

00:16:51.061 --> 00:16:54.498
you actually have a distillation
of all the worst problems

00:16:54.498 --> 00:16:55.748
in the company.

00:16:56.920 --> 00:16:58.401
So, there's no point in spending your time

00:16:58.401 --> 00:17:00.175
on things that are going right.

00:17:00.175 --> 00:17:03.619
So, you only spend your time
on things that are going wrong

00:17:03.619 --> 00:17:05.146
and there are things that are going wrong

00:17:05.146 --> 00:17:07.371
that some other people can't take care of,

00:17:07.371 --> 00:17:09.058
so you're like the worst.

00:17:09.058 --> 00:17:11.666
You have a filter for the
crappiest problems in the company,

00:17:11.666 --> 00:17:12.851
(laughing)

00:17:12.851 --> 00:17:16.869
the most pernicious and painful problems.

00:17:16.869 --> 00:17:18.077
So, I wouldn't say, it's,

00:17:18.077 --> 00:17:22.384
I think you have to feel
quite compelled to do it

00:17:22.384 --> 00:17:24.763
and have a fairly high pain threshold.

00:17:24.763 --> 00:17:27.119
There's a friend of mine who
says like starting a company

00:17:27.119 --> 00:17:30.952
is like staring at the
abyss and eating glass.

00:17:32.055 --> 00:17:33.768
And there's some truth to that.

00:17:33.768 --> 00:17:36.337
(audience laughing)

00:17:36.337 --> 00:17:37.212
The staring into the abyss part

00:17:37.212 --> 00:17:38.721
is that you're going
to be constantly facing

00:17:38.721 --> 00:17:42.888
the extermination of the company,
cause most startups fail.

00:17:45.417 --> 00:17:49.650
It's like 90% or 99% of startups fail.

00:17:49.650 --> 00:17:53.520
So, that's the staring
into the abyss part.

00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:57.728
You're constantly saying,
"If I don't get this right,

00:17:57.728 --> 00:17:59.500
"the company will die."

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:02.369
Should be quite stressful.

00:18:02.369 --> 00:18:05.119
And then the eating glass part is

00:18:05.969 --> 00:18:08.386
you've got to do the problems,

00:18:09.272 --> 00:18:10.482
you're going to work on the problems

00:18:10.482 --> 00:18:11.650
that the company needs you to work on,

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:13.591
not the problems you want to work on.

00:18:13.591 --> 00:18:17.822
And so, that's, you end
up working on problems

00:18:17.822 --> 00:18:20.762
that you really wish
you weren't working on

00:18:20.762 --> 00:18:24.654
and so, that's the eating glass part.

00:18:24.654 --> 00:18:26.225
And that goes on for a long time.

00:18:26.225 --> 00:18:27.844
- [Interviewer] So, how
you do you keep your focus

00:18:27.844 --> 00:18:30.803
on the big picture when
you're constantly faced with,

00:18:30.803 --> 00:18:34.220
"We could be out of business in a month?"

00:18:35.568 --> 00:18:37.073
- Well, it's just a very small percentage

00:18:37.073 --> 00:18:40.130
of mental energy that's
on the big picture.

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:43.560
Like, you generally know
where you're heading for

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:45.552
and the actual path is going to be

00:18:45.552 --> 00:18:49.843
some sort of zig-zaggy
thing in that direction.

00:18:49.843 --> 00:18:51.807
You try not to deviate
too far from the path

00:18:51.807 --> 00:18:54.144
that you want to be on

00:18:54.144 --> 00:18:58.564
but you're going to have
to do that to some degree.

00:18:58.564 --> 00:18:59.987
But I don't want to diminish,

00:18:59.987 --> 00:19:03.741
I think the probability is a good one

00:19:03.741 --> 00:19:06.576
if the rules of an industry
are properly set up.

00:19:06.576 --> 00:19:08.100
So, there's nothing
fundamentally wrong with profit.

00:19:08.100 --> 00:19:11.303
In fact, profit just means
that people are paying you more

00:19:11.303 --> 00:19:15.800
for whatever you're doing than
you're spending to create it.

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:17.380
That's a good thing.

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:18.453
(laughing)

00:19:18.453 --> 00:19:20.743
And if that's not the case,

00:19:20.743 --> 00:19:24.688
then you'll be out of
business and rightfully so.

00:19:24.688 --> 00:19:26.828
You're not adding enough value.

00:19:26.828 --> 00:19:30.104
- There's a latin expression,
which I think is great.

00:19:30.104 --> 00:19:33.702
I love it, it was in a now very old movie

00:19:33.702 --> 00:19:36.740
called The Dead Poet's Society,

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:40.907
but the line in the movie was
carpe diem, seize the day.

00:19:41.959 --> 00:19:45.027
The opportunities are there
but you've got to reach out

00:19:45.027 --> 00:19:46.755
and pick them up.

00:19:46.755 --> 00:19:48.809
You've got to grab at 'em.

00:19:48.809 --> 00:19:51.063
Some of you may have
already done that at the U

00:19:51.063 --> 00:19:54.908
and your classes, in other
students that you met,

00:19:54.908 --> 00:19:58.622
in your extracurricular
activities, but grab them.

00:19:58.622 --> 00:20:00.224
Don't be afraid to make a mistake

00:20:00.224 --> 00:20:03.885
because you know what you can
do if you grab the wrong one?

00:20:03.885 --> 00:20:06.058
Drop it and pick up another one.

00:20:06.058 --> 00:20:08.141
It's okay, seize the day.

00:20:10.090 --> 00:20:12.840
I think back, of all of the luck,

00:20:14.177 --> 00:20:16.829
but also the times that
were in front of me,

00:20:16.829 --> 00:20:18.246
to seize the day.

00:20:19.099 --> 00:20:22.644
I don't know what got me to
drop out of business school

00:20:22.644 --> 00:20:24.636
and come to Microsoft.

00:20:24.636 --> 00:20:27.840
My parents thought I was a whack job.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:30.140
Neither one of them graduated college

00:20:30.140 --> 00:20:33.804
and they thought this
was really a wild idea.

00:20:33.804 --> 00:20:36.304
I was lucky, I seized the day.

00:20:37.528 --> 00:20:41.195
Microsoft, one day some
guys fly in from IBM

00:20:43.478 --> 00:20:44.916
and all of a sudden, we figure out

00:20:44.916 --> 00:20:47.349
that we could actually
provide all the software

00:20:47.349 --> 00:20:48.919
they need for this thing

00:20:48.919 --> 00:20:52.328
that became the personal computer.

00:20:52.328 --> 00:20:55.364
People, Bill, Paul, they had the wisdom

00:20:55.364 --> 00:20:59.806
to seize the day when that
opportunity presented itself

00:20:59.806 --> 00:21:02.025
and yet, when you think
of all the opportunities,

00:21:02.025 --> 00:21:04.735
when I think back of all
the opportunities I've had,

00:21:04.735 --> 00:21:07.106
the one that was most important

00:21:07.106 --> 00:21:10.451
was an opportunity I got in 1969.

00:21:10.451 --> 00:21:12.954
Sitting in my junior high school class,

00:21:12.954 --> 00:21:14.960
I was in a public junior high,

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:17.329
not very stimulated at the time,

00:21:17.329 --> 00:21:20.162
and over the loudspeaker they said

00:21:21.212 --> 00:21:23.630
that a private high school in our area

00:21:23.630 --> 00:21:25.364
that I'd never heard of

00:21:25.364 --> 00:21:29.023
was giving scholarship tests that weekend.

00:21:29.023 --> 00:21:30.849
I told my mother I wanted to take 'em,

00:21:30.849 --> 00:21:33.243
she said, "That's fine as
long as you get a scholarship

00:21:33.243 --> 00:21:36.084
"because we can't afford
to send you there."

00:21:36.084 --> 00:21:38.655
But that's really where I got switched on.

00:21:38.655 --> 00:21:41.678
Switched on in math,
switched on personally,

00:21:41.678 --> 00:21:45.845
energized in a way that never,
never could be turned back.

00:21:46.789 --> 00:21:50.440
And I'm very thankful
for that opportunity.

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:53.771
Yes, there's a lot of luck in opportunity

00:21:53.771 --> 00:21:56.962
but there's a lot of seizing the day

00:21:56.962 --> 00:22:01.512
and I encourage you all to
reach out and carpe diem,

00:22:01.512 --> 00:22:05.430
really seize the opportunities
that are in front of you.

00:22:05.430 --> 00:22:09.670
- I've been working, I've
been in business for 69 years,

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:12.170
since I was 12 and every time,

00:22:13.145 --> 00:22:16.363
well, after the first
dozen or so businesses,

00:22:16.363 --> 00:22:18.713
I realized that the only way to get ahead

00:22:18.713 --> 00:22:23.697
was to do things differently
than the way it was being done.

00:22:23.697 --> 00:22:27.780
And then I came to learn,
which I finally learned

00:22:29.925 --> 00:22:34.092
at my ripe young age, that
if you do things differently,

00:22:37.313 --> 00:22:40.986
success will follow you like your shadow

00:22:40.986 --> 00:22:43.328
and you can't get rid of it.

00:22:43.328 --> 00:22:47.638
So, that's what I did,
so people doubted me

00:22:47.638 --> 00:22:51.471
because people just
didn't do what I would do.

00:22:53.293 --> 00:22:56.271
It's a matter of finding out what it is

00:22:56.271 --> 00:23:00.786
that an industry does and
identifying the opportunity

00:23:00.786 --> 00:23:03.134
as to how to do it differently

00:23:03.134 --> 00:23:05.721
and then being enough of a risk taker

00:23:05.721 --> 00:23:08.203
to actually do it differently.

00:23:08.203 --> 00:23:09.835
So, people doubted me

00:23:09.835 --> 00:23:13.590
because nobody thought of
doing it the way that I did

00:23:13.590 --> 00:23:17.026
and nobody had, they didn't have the,

00:23:17.026 --> 00:23:19.318
they were satisfied with the status quo

00:23:19.318 --> 00:23:21.807
and I'm never satisfied
with the status quo.

